"""Q&A data for generate_remaining.py (7 x 50 questions)."""

sd_prereq = [
    ("What is caching and why is it essential in system design?",
     " Caching stores frequently accessed data in fast storage (memory) closer to the application so repeated reads avoid slow disk or network calls. It reduces latency, lowers database load, and improves throughput. Every large product relies on caching at browser, Content Delivery Network (CDN), application, and database layers."),
    ("What are the main types of caches in a web application?",
     " Common layers: client browser cache, Content Delivery Network (CDN) for static assets, application cache (Redis or Memcached), database query cache, and CPU cache. Each trades freshness for speed. Mention at least two layers and what each stores in interviews."),
    ("What is a cache hit vs a cache miss?",
     " A cache hit finds data in cache (fast read). A cache miss requires fetching from origin database or API (slow). Hit ratio equals hits divided by total requests—a key metric. Target 90% or higher on hot paths."),
    ("What is cache eviction and name common policies?",
     " Eviction removes entries when cache is full. Least Recently Used (LRU) evicts oldest accessed item. Least Frequently Used (LFU) evicts least popular. First In First Out (FIFO) evicts oldest inserted. Time To Live (TTL) expires by time. LRU is most common."),
    ("What is cache-aside (lazy loading) pattern?",
     " Application checks cache first. On miss, reads database, returns to client, writes cache. Application manages both tiers. Most widely used pattern—simple and caches only actually-read data."),
    ("What is write-through caching?",
     " Every write goes to cache and database synchronously. Reads are fast and consistent. Downside: double write latency. Use when read consistency matters and write volume is moderate."),
    ("What is write-back (write-behind) caching?",
     " Write to cache immediately; flush to database asynchronously. Very fast writes but risk data loss if cache crashes before flush. Use for write-heavy workloads with acceptable brief inconsistency."),
    ("What is read-through caching?",
     " Cache loads from database on miss—application only talks to cache. Simplifies code but needs cache library support. Pairs well with write-through for managed cache tier."),
    ("What is the thundering herd problem in caching?",
     " When hot key expires, many requests miss simultaneously and overload database. Fix with probabilistic early expiration, single-flight rebuild, stale-while-revalidate, or pre-warming before spikes."),
    ("What is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) and how does it cache?",
     " CDN is geographically distributed edge servers caching static content near users. Edge serves cached assets; on miss fetches from origin once. Reduces global latency and protects origin from spikes."),
    ("What is Redis and why is it popular for caching?",
     " Redis is in-memory data store supporting strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, pub/sub. Sub-millisecond latency and Time To Live (TTL) make it ideal for sessions, rate limits, leaderboards, and hot keys."),
    ("What is Memcached vs Redis?",
     " Memcached: simple multi-threaded key-value cache, horizontal scale. Redis: richer data structures, persistence, pub/sub, scripting. Pick Memcached for pure cache; Redis for advanced features."),
    ("What is cache stampede and how do you prevent it?",
     " Many clients recompute expired hot key at once. Prevent with single-flight (one rebuilds, others wait), lock with timeout, or background refresh before expiry."),
    ("What is a load balancer and why do you need one?",
     " Distributes traffic across backend servers. Improves availability, enables horizontal scaling, can terminate Secure Sockets Layer (SSL). Standard in front of every production server pool."),
    ("What is the difference between Layer 4 and Layer 7 load balancing?",
     " Layer 4 routes by IP and Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) port—fast, no HTTP inspection. Layer 7 routes by URL path or headers—smarter routing, slightly higher latency. Application Load Balancers use Layer 7."),
    ("What are common load balancing algorithms?",
     " Round robin, weighted round robin, least connections, IP hash for stickiness, and consistent hashing to minimize remapping on topology changes."),
    ("What is health checking in load balancers?",
     " Periodic pings (HTTP /health or TCP) detect dead backends and remove them from rotation. Tune interval and thresholds to avoid flapping or routing to failed servers."),
    ("What is session stickiness (session affinity)?",
     " Same client always hits same backend to preserve in-memory session. Better: store sessions in Redis for stateless servers that scale horizontally."),
    ("What is the CAP theorem?",
     " CAP (Consistency, Availability, Partition tolerance): during network partition, choose Consistency or Availability. Partition tolerance is mandatory in distributed systems. Tune per use case."),
    ("What is the PACELC theorem extension of CAP?",
     " If Partition choose A or C; Else choose Latency or Consistency. Reflects real trade-offs beyond partitions—e.g., DynamoDB favors availability and low latency."),
    ("What is strong consistency vs eventual consistency?",
     " Strong: reads always see latest write. Eventual: replicas converge over time, reads may be stale temporarily. Strong for money; eventual for likes and view counts."),
    ("What is a network partition in distributed systems?",
     " Nodes cannot communicate, splitting into isolated groups. Must choose serving stale data (availability) or rejecting requests (consistency) until healed."),
    ("How do you do back-of-the-envelope estimation for system design?",
     " Compute users × actions/day ÷ 86400 for Queries Per Second (QPS). Plan 3–5× peak. Show structured math—approach matters more than exact numbers."),
    ("How do you estimate storage requirements?",
     " Objects/day × size × retention × replica factor. Add index overhead (~30–50%). Round up. Example: 1M photos/day × 2MB × 365 × 3 replicas."),
    ("How do you estimate bandwidth requirements?",
     " QPS × average response size. Add upload separately. Compare to link capacity; offload static assets to Content Delivery Network (CDN)."),
    ("What is the 80/20 rule in caching?",
     " Roughly 80% of requests hit 20% of keys (hot data). Cache the hot 20%. Monitor access patterns; over-caching cold keys wastes memory."),
    ("What is cache warming?",
     " Preload cache before traffic (post-deploy, pre-sale). Batch load hot keys, use single-flight on first access, or CDN prefetch. Avoids cold-start latency spikes."),
    ("What is negative caching?",
     " Cache 'not found' results with short TTL to prevent repeated database lookups for missing keys. Short TTL avoids stale absence after create."),
    ("What is a Bloom filter and how does it relate to caching?",
     " Probabilistic set membership test—may false positive, never false negative. Skip lookup if definitely absent. Saves disk/network on negative lookups."),
    ("What is consistent hashing?",
     " Maps keys and nodes to ring; add/remove node only remaps adjacent range. Used in caches, sharding, load balancing. Virtual nodes balance load."),
    ("What is a reverse proxy vs a load balancer?",
     " Reverse proxy forwards requests, may cache and compress. Load balancer distributes across backends. Nginx and HAProxy often do both plus SSL termination."),
    ("What is autoscaling and how does it relate to load balancing?",
     " Adds/removes instances on CPU, QPS, or queue depth. Load balancer picks up new instances from registry. Cooldown prevents flapping."),
    ("What is a single point of failure (SPOF)?",
     " One component failure downs entire system. Mitigate with replication, multi-Availability Zone deployment, redundant load balancers."),
    ("What is latency vs throughput?",
     " Latency: time per operation. Throughput: operations per second. Caching lowers latency and raises throughput by offloading database."),
    ("What is p50, p95, and p99 latency?",
     " Percentiles of response time. p99 captures tail latency affecting many users at scale. Service Level Objectives often target p99 under 200ms."),
    ("What is an Availability Zone (AZ) in cloud?",
     " Isolated datacenter within region. Multi-AZ deployment survives zone failure. Amazon Web Services (AWS) regions have multiple zones."),
    ("What is the difference between vertical and horizontal scaling?",
     " Vertical: bigger machine, limited ceiling. Horizontal: more machines, needs load balancing and distributed data. Web scale uses horizontal."),
    ("What is a hot key problem in distributed caches?",
     " One key gets extreme traffic, overloading a shard. Fix with local cache, replicate key, or split key space."),
    ("What is cache coherency?",
     " Keeping all cache layers consistent after writes. Use pub/sub invalidation, short TTL, or avoid local cache for volatile data."),
    ("What is estimation for read vs write ratio?",
     " Many apps are 90%+ reads. Drives read replicas, caching, CDN. Ask interviewer read/write split—it shapes architecture."),
    ("What is QPS and how do you calculate peak QPS?",
     " Queries Per Second = daily requests / 86400. Peak ≈ average × 2–5. Size infrastructure for peak."),
    ("What is DAU and MAU in estimation?",
     " Daily Active Users and Monthly Active Users. DAU/MAU shows stickiness. DAU × actions/day = daily volume."),
    ("What is a SLA, SLO, and SLI?",
     " Service Level Agreement (customer contract), Service Level Objective (internal target), Service Level Indicator (measured metric). Error budget guides release risk."),
    ("Why is estimation important before choosing cache size?",
     " Right-size cache for working set. Too small thrashes; too large wastes money. Monitor hit ratio and memory."),
    ("What is edge caching vs origin caching?",
     " Edge CDN for static assets globally. Origin Redis near app for dynamic API data. Use both layers."),
    ("What is cache invalidation and why is it hard?",
     " Coordinating stale data across CDN, Redis, and local caches on writes. Hard due to races and multiple layers. Use TTL plus event invalidation."),
    ("What is a typical caching architecture for a read-heavy API?",
     " CDN → Load Balancer → App → Redis cache-aside → DB with read replicas. Invalidate Redis on write."),
    ("How do you explain trade-offs in a system design prerequisites interview?",
     " Frame as trade-offs: consistency vs latency, memory vs hit ratio. No single correct answer—show structured reasoning."),
    ("What is Moore's Law relevance to estimation?",
     " Hardware improves but traffic often grows faster. Estimate current scale plus 2–3 year growth."),
    ("Summarize the system design prerequisites checklist.",
     " Master caching, load balancing, CAP theorem, and estimation math. Practice Twitter/Instagram/URL shortener scale. Spell out abbreviations."),
]

assert len(sd_prereq) == 50

